0,151645,198,151644,77091,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nWhat is the least positive integer $n$ such that 1560 divides $n!$?","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"15f2b8afa1072cf60688331545d0694f2c4d756ed1ec5b584ef351763e07ff5c"},"seconds":27.27614736557007,"seed":239652787833496991,"stop":"eos","text":"To determine the least positive integer \\( n \\) such that \\( 1560 \\) divides \\( n! \\), we start by finding the prime factorization of \\( 1560 \\).\n\nFirst, we perform the factorization:\n\\[\n1560 \\div 2 = 780 \\\\\n780 \\div 2 = 390 \\\\\n390 \\div 2 = 195 \\\\\n195 \\div 3 = 65 \\\\\n65 \\div 5 = 13 \\\\\n13 \\div 13 = 1\n\\]\nSo, the prime factorization of \\( 1560 \\) is:\n\\[\n1560 = 2^3 \\times 3 \\times 5 \\times 13\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the smallest \\( n \\) such that \\( n! \\) contains at least these prime factors. We need to check the factorials until we have at least three factors of 2, one factor of 3, one factor of 5, and one factor of 13.\n\n- The number of factors of 2 in \\( n! \\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{2} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{4} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{8} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{16} \\right\\rfloor + \\cdots\n\\]\n- The number of factors of 3 in \\( n! \\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{3} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{9} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{27} \\right\\rfloor + \\cdots\n\\]\n- The number of factors of 5 in \\( n! \\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{5} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{25} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{125} \\right\\rfloor + \\cdots\n\\]\n- The number of factors of 13 in \\( n! \\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{13} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{169} \\right\\rfloor + \\cdots\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the smallest \\( n \\) such that:\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{2} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{4} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{8} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{16} \\right\\rfloor + \\cdots \\geq 3\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{3} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{9} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{27} \\right\\rfloor + \\cdots \\geq 1\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{5} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{25} \\right\\rfloor + \\cdots \\geq 1\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{13} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{169} \\right\\rfloor + \\cdots \\geq 1\n\\]\n\nLet's check \\( n = 13 \\):\n- Factors of 2: \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{13}{2} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{13}{4} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{13}{8} \\right\\rfloor = 6 + 3 + 1 = 10 \\geq 3 \\)\n- Factors of 3: \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{13}{3} \\right\\rfloor + \\left\\lfloor \\frac{13}{9} \\right\\rfloor = 4 + 1 = 5 \\geq 1 \\)\n- Factors of 5: \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{13}{5} \\right\\rfloor = 2 \\geq 1 \\)\n- Factors of 13: \\( \\left\\lfloor \\frac{13}{13} \\right\\rfloor = 1 \\geq 1 \\)\n\nSince all conditions are satisfied for \\( n = 13 \\), the smallest \\( n \\) such that \\( 1560 \\) divides \\( n! \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{13}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{13}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"number_theory:23"}
